{"id":"26193","title":"Zephaniah 3:7","from":[3,7],"to":[3,7],"text":"rose early: The people of Jerusalem eagerly got out of bed to commit more evil deeds (compare Proverbs 1:16). They had strayed far from the path of their ancestor Abraham, who rose early to follow God's command (Genesis 22:3)."},{"id":"26194","title":"Zephaniah 3:8","from":[3,8],"to":[3,8],"text":"wait for Me: See Habakkuk 2:3. rise to testify: The prophet describes a courtroom scene. God first acts as a witness (see also Jeremiah 29:23; Malachi 3:5) on his own behalf, then serves as judge (see also Job 9:15; Psalm 50:6) to give his fair sentence. The fire of my jealousy refers to the Lord’s rightful anger against sin. It also shows his care for his holy name and his people's well-being (see Isaiah 66:13–16)."},{"id":"26197","title":"Zephaniah 3:9","from":[3,9],"to":[3,9],"text":"God plans for the blessings promised to the faithful remnant in Zephaniah 3:9–13 to reach people from every nation. Not just Israel, but all people will transform, call on the Lord (see Isaiah 55:5), and serve him (see Isaiah 59:19–21; Zechariah 14:16). Spreading the good news to all nations helped fulfill this vision (Matthew 28:19–20; Romans 10:9–13)."},{"id":"26195","title":"Zephaniah 3:9–13","from":[3,9],"to":[3,13],"text":"The bright future described in these verses gives more reason to wait patiently for the Lord (Zephaniah 3:8). Like the prophets Isaiah, Nahum, and Habakkuk, Zephaniah uses judgment and hope as main themes."},{"id":"26196","title":"Zephaniah 3:9–20","from":[3,9],"to":[3,20],"text":"God shows his plans for a humble and pure group of his people (Zephaniah 3:9–13). He encourages them to rejoice in the coming abundant blessings from their saving Lord (Zephaniah 3:14–20)."},{"id":"26181","title":"Zephaniah 3:10","from":[3,10],"to":[3,10],"text":"The rivers of Ethiopia are the distant sources of the Nile River. In ancient times, people saw the Nile's origin as a great mystery. This expression refers to the farthest parts of the earth."},{"id":"26182","title":"Zephaniah 3:12","from":[3,12],"to":[3,12],"text":"Jesus the Messiah, God's chosen one, perfectly shows the moral qualities expected of the faithful people of Israel (Isaiah 42:1–4; 53:3, 7–9; Zechariah 9:9; see Matthew 11:28–30; 12:15–21; Philippians 2:1–8; 1 Peter 2:23)."},{"id":"26183","title":"Zephaniah 3:14","from":[3,14],"to":[3,14],"text":"Sing … shout aloud … Be glad and rejoice!: These commands together show that God’s people will one day experience very great joy."},{"id":"26184","title":"Zephaniah 3:15","from":[3,15],"to":[3,15],"text":"The true king of Israel was always meant to be the Lord (Numbers 23:21; 1 Samuel 8:7; Isaiah 44:6). is among you: See also Isaiah 54:4–8; 57:14–19; 62:10–12; Ezekiel 48:35; Joel 3:17, 21."},{"id":"26185","title":"Zephaniah 3:17","from":[3,17],"to":[3,17],"text":"mighty to save: God served as Israel’s divine warrior and redeemer (see Psalm 24:8–10; Isaiah 42:13; Habakkuk 3:8–15). One of God’s titles is “the Mighty God” (Isaiah 10:21). This title also refers to the Messiah (Isaiah 9:6). He will rejoice over you: Jerusalem and all Israel will rejoice in God (Zephaniah 3:14), and God will rejoice over them as a purified and faithful people (see 3:12–13)."},{"id":"26187","title":"Zephaniah 3:18","from":[3,18],"to":[3,18],"text":"I will gather … no longer suffer reproach: The Lord's promise is very different from the warnings at the start of the book. First, God threatened to gather the nations to remove the people of Judah from the earth (Zephaniah 1:2–4). Now, he promises to gather those exiled from Jerusalem and lead them safely home."},{"id":"26186","title":"Zephaniah 3:18–20","from":[3,18],"to":[3,20],"text":"The repeated use of I will in these verses emphasises God's promises to his people."},{"id":"26188","title":"Zephaniah 3:19","from":[3,19],"to":[3,19],"text":"God will change his people's past shame into glory and fame (see Deuteronomy 26:19; Isaiah 62:7; Micah 4:6–8; compare 1 Peter 5:4)."},{"id":"26190","title":"Zephaniah 3:20","from":[3,20],"to":[3,20],"text":"At that time: After a period of severe anger and judgment, the day of the Lord ends with eternal blessings for everyone who trusts in him (Zephaniah 3:9, 12; see Psalm 2:12; Isaiah 45:22; see also Revelation 19:11–22:5). says the LORD: Zephaniah’s prophecy, entirely from God (see also Zephaniah 1:1–3, 10; 2:9; 3:8), is completely trustworthy."}],"terms":{"1":[["ה֥וֹי","Ho","woe to!",""],["מֹרְאָ֖ה","mo.re.'Ah","[the] filthy [one]",""],["וְ/נִגְאָלָ֑ה","ve./nig.'a.Lah","and/ [the] defiled [one]",""],["הָ/עִ֖יר","ha./'Ir","the/ city",""],["הַ/יּוֹנָֽה\\׃","hai./yo.Nah","which/ oppresses",""]],"2":[["לֹ֤א","lo'","not",""],["שָֽׁמְעָה֙","sha.me.'Ah","it has listened",""],["בְּ/ק֔וֹל","be./Kol","to/ a voice",""],["לֹ֥א","lo'","not",""],["לָקְחָ֖ה","la.ke.Chah","it has accepted",""],["מוּסָ֑ר","mu.Sar","correction",""],["בַּֽ/יהוָה֙","ba./Yah.weh","in/ Yahweh",""],["לֹ֣א","lo'","not",""],["בָטָ֔חָה","va.Ta.chah","it has trusted",""],["אֶל\\־","'el-","to",""],["אֱלֹהֶ֖י/הָ","'e.lo.Hei./ha","God/ its",""],["לֹ֥א","lo'","not",""],["קָרֵֽבָה\\׃","ka.Re.vah","it has drawn near",""]],"3":[["שָׂרֶ֣י/הָ","sa.Rei./ha","officials/ its",""],["בְ/קִרְבָּ֔/הּ","ve./kir.Ba/h","in/ midst/ its",""],["אֲרָי֖וֹת","'a.ra.Yot","[are] lions",""],["שֹֽׁאֲגִ֑ים","sho.'a.Gim","roaring",""],["שֹׁפְטֶ֙י/הָ֙","sho.fe.Tei./ha","judges/ its",""],["זְאֵ֣בֵי","ze.'E.vei","[are] wolves of",""],["עֶ֔רֶב","'E.rev","[the] evening",""],["לֹ֥א","lo'","[which] not",""],["גָרְמ֖וּ","ga.re.Mu","they gnaw",""],["לַ/בֹּֽקֶר\\׃","la./Bo.ker","to the/ morning",""]],"4":[["נְבִיאֶ֙י/הָ֙","ne.vi.'Ei./ha","prophets/ its",""],["פֹּֽחֲזִ֔ים","po.cha.Zim","[are] insolent",""],["אַנְשֵׁ֖י","'an.Shei","men of",""],["בֹּֽגְד֑וֹת","bo.ge.Dot","treacheri<es>",""],["כֹּהֲנֶ֙י/הָ֙","ko.ha.Nei./ha","priests/ its",""],["חִלְּלוּ\\־","chi.le.lu-","they profane",""],["קֹ֔דֶשׁ","Ko.desh","[the] sanctuary",""],["חָמְס֖וּ","cha.me.Su",